    "content": "Over 3.1 million bottles of over-the-counter eye drops manufactured by K.C. Pharmaceuticals are being recalled due to concerns over a lack of sterility assurance. The products, which could cause temporary, reversible eye infections, were sold at major retailers including CVS, Walgreens, Rite Aid, Walmart, H-E-B, and Kroger. Recalled Products and Brands (Sold under these labels): Dry Eye Relief Eye Drops (Major recalled item) Artificial Tears Sterile Lubricant Eye Drops Advanced Relief Eye Drops Redness Lubricant Drops Sterile Eye Drops (Various types: AC, Original Formula, Soothing Tears) Store Brands Involved: CVS Health, Walgreens, Rite Aid, Equaline, Leader, H-E-B, Kroger, Best Choice, Quality Choice, Meijer, and Dollar General. What to Do: Check your eye drops for the brand names and product types listed above. Review the FDA's full list of NDC (National Drug Code) or UPC numbers, which are typically found near the barcode, to confirm if your product is affected. FDA's full list Immediately stop using the products and return them to the store for a refund. If you have experienced eye pain, redness, or vision changes, contact a healthcare provider. Key Details: Manufacturer: K.C. Pharmaceuticals, Inc. (Pomona, California) Recall Class: Class II (means the product may cause temporary or reversible adverse health consequences). Affected Expiration Dates: Many affected products have expiration dates between May 2026 and October 2026. Disclaimer: This information is based on reports as of early April 2026.",
